from extras import ex

ex('digestion',"Carbohydrase breaks starch into sugars, protease breaks proteins into amino acids, and lipase breaks fats into fatty acids and glycerol. Small molecules are absorbed through the wall of the small intestine. Villi give the small intestine a huge surface area.",
 [('Which enzyme breaks down protein?','Protease.','Lipase.','Carbohydrase.','Protease → amino acids.'),
  ('Where are most digested food molecules absorbed into the blood?','Small intestine.','Stomach.','Large intestine.','Villi absorb small molecules.'),
  ('Why do villi help absorption?','They increase the surface area.','They make food move faster.','They produce acid.','More surface means faster absorption.'),
  ('What is the main job of the large intestine?','Absorbing water.','Digesting protein.','Making bile.','Water is taken back into the body.')],
 [('What does an enzyme do in digestion?','Speeds up the breakdown of large food molecules into small ones.'),
  ('Name the products when fats are digested.','Fatty acids.~Glycerol.'),
  ('Explain why starch must be digested before it can be used by the body.','Starch molecules are too large to pass through the gut wall.~Carbohydrase breaks them into small sugar molecules.~Sugars dissolve and are absorbed into the blood.')])

ex('sound',"Sound travels fastest in solids and cannot travel through a vacuum. Frequency is the number of vibrations per second, measured in hertz, Hz. Higher frequency gives a higher pitch; bigger amplitude gives a louder sound.",
 [('Why can sound not travel through space?','There are no particles to pass on the vibrations.','It is too cold.','Space is too dark.','Sound needs a medium.'),
  ('A higher frequency sound has a…','higher pitch.','lower pitch.','quieter volume.','Pitch depends on frequency.'),
  ('A bigger amplitude makes a sound…','louder.','higher.','faster.','Amplitude relates to loudness.'),
  ('In which material does sound usually travel fastest?','Steel (a solid).','Air (a gas).','Water (a liquid).','Particles in solids are close together.')],
 [('What is the unit of frequency?','Hertz (Hz).'),
  ('A guitar string is plucked harder. Describe what happens to the wave and the sound.','The amplitude of the vibration increases.~The sound is louder (pitch stays the same).'),
  ('Explain why an astronaut on a spacewalk cannot hear a nearby explosion.','Sound is passed on by vibrating particles.~Space is a vacuum with almost no particles.~So the vibrations cannot travel to the astronaut.')])

ex('ear',"Humans hear frequencies from about 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz. Loud sounds can damage the tiny hairs in the cochlea, causing hearing loss. An echo is a reflected sound; echoes can be used to measure distance.",
 [('Which part of the ear first vibrates when sound arrives?','Eardrum.','Cochlea.','Auditory nerve.','The eardrum is at the end of the ear canal.'),
  ('What is the normal human hearing range?','About 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz.','About 1 Hz to 100 Hz.','Above 50,000 Hz only.','Ultrasound is above 20,000 Hz.'),
  ('What is an echo?','A reflected sound.','A very quiet sound.','A sound in a vacuum.','Sound bounces off hard surfaces.'),
  ('Why should you wear ear defenders near loud machines?','Loud sounds can damage hair cells in the cochlea.','They keep your ears warm.','They make sounds louder.','Damage can cause permanent hearing loss.')],
 [('What does the cochlea do?','Changes vibrations into electrical signals (nerve impulses) for the brain.'),
  ('A clap echoes from a wall 85 m away after 0.5 s. Calculate the speed of sound.','Distance travelled = 2 × 85 = 170 m.~Speed = 170 ÷ 0.5 = 340 m/s.'),
  ('Describe the path of a sound from outside the ear to the brain.','The outer ear funnels sound down the ear canal to the eardrum, which vibrates.~The ossicles pass on and amplify the vibrations to the cochlea.~The cochlea makes nerve impulses that travel along the auditory nerve to the brain.')])

ex('light',"The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, measured from the normal. Light slows down when it enters glass and bends towards the normal. Light travels at about 300,000 km/s, much faster than sound.",
 [('For a plane mirror, the angle of reflection is…','equal to the angle of incidence.','always 90°.','half the angle of incidence.','This is the law of reflection.'),
  ('When light enters glass from air it bends…','towards the normal.','away from the normal.','not at all, at any angle.','It slows down in glass.'),
  ('Why do you see lightning before you hear thunder?','Light travels much faster than sound.','Sound travels faster.','Thunder happens later.','Light reaches you almost instantly.'),
  ('The normal is a line drawn…','at 90° to the surface.','along the surface.','at 45° to the surface.','Angles are measured from the normal.')],
 [('What is refraction?','The change in direction of light as it passes from one material into another.'),
  ('A ray hits a mirror with an angle of incidence of 35°. State the angle of reflection and between which lines it is measured.','35°.~Between the reflected ray and the normal.'),
  ('Explain why a straw looks bent in a glass of water.','Light from the straw refracts as it leaves the water into the air.~It changes direction at the surface.~Your brain assumes light travels in straight lines, so the straw appears in a different place.')])

ex('colour',"A red object looks red because it reflects red light and absorbs other colours. A red filter only lets red light through. A convex lens focuses light to a point; the eye uses a lens to focus light onto the retina.",
 [('What colour does a blue shirt look in red light?','Black.','Blue.','Red.','It absorbs red and there is no blue light to reflect.'),
  ('White light can be split into colours by…','a prism.','a mirror.','a filter only.','Different colours refract by different amounts.'),
  ('A green filter lets through…','green light only.','all colours except green.','red light only.','Filters transmit their own colour.'),
  ('Which type of lens brings light rays together?','Convex.','Concave.','Flat.','Convex lenses focus light.')],
 [('What is dispersion?','The splitting of white light into a spectrum of colours.'),
  ('White light shines through a red filter onto a green apple. State and explain the colour the apple appears.','Black.~Only red light reaches it and a green apple absorbs red light, reflecting none.'),
  ('Explain why grass looks green in sunlight.','Sunlight contains all colours.~Grass absorbs most colours.~It reflects green light into our eyes.')])

ex('combustion',"Complete combustion of a fuel makes carbon dioxide and water. Limewater turns milky when carbon dioxide is bubbled through it. Heating copper carbonate breaks it down into copper oxide and carbon dioxide.",
 [('What is made when methane burns completely in oxygen?','Carbon dioxide and water.','Carbon monoxide only.','Hydrogen and oxygen.','Fuel + oxygen → CO₂ + H₂O.'),
  ('What is the test for carbon dioxide?','It turns limewater milky.','It relights a glowing splint.','It gives a squeaky pop.','Limewater goes cloudy.'),
  ('Thermal decomposition means…','breaking down a substance using heat.','burning in oxygen.','dissolving in water.','One reactant breaks into two or more products.'),
  ('Which three things does a fire need?','Fuel, oxygen and heat.','Water, fuel and light.','Oxygen, water and heat.','This is the fire triangle.')],
 [('What gas is needed for combustion?','Oxygen.'),
  ('Write the word equation for heating copper carbonate.','Copper carbonate → copper oxide…~… + carbon dioxide.'),
  ('Explain how a fire blanket puts out a small fire.','A fire needs fuel, heat and oxygen.~The blanket covers the fire and stops oxygen reaching it.~Without oxygen the fire goes out.')])

ex('mass',"When magnesium burns, the product is heavier because oxygen atoms from the air join it. When a gas escapes from an open flask, the mass seems to decrease. In a sealed container, the total mass stays exactly the same.",
 [('Why does mass increase when magnesium burns in air?','Oxygen from the air combines with it.','Atoms are created.','Magnesium gets hotter.','The product includes the added oxygen.'),
  ('In an open flask, marble chips react with acid and the mass goes down because…','carbon dioxide gas escapes.','atoms are destroyed.','the acid evaporates instantly.','The gas leaves the container.'),
  ('What happens to total mass in a sealed container during a reaction?','It stays the same.','It increases.','It decreases.','No atoms can enter or leave.'),
  ('Conservation of mass means…','atoms are not created or destroyed in reactions.','mass always increases.','gases have no mass.','Atoms are just rearranged.')],
 [('What is the law of conservation of mass?','Mass is not created or destroyed in a chemical reaction.'),
  ('24 g of magnesium reacts with 16 g of oxygen. Calculate the mass of magnesium oxide and explain how you know.','24 + 16 = 40 g.~All the atoms from the reactants end up in the product, so mass is conserved.'),
  ('Explain why the mass in an open beaker decreases when an acid reacts with a carbonate.','The reaction makes carbon dioxide gas.~The gas escapes into the air.~Its mass is no longer measured, but the total mass of all substances is still conserved.')])

ex('chemical-energy',"Burning, neutralisation and hand warmers are exothermic. Thermal decomposition and dissolving some salts, like in cold packs, are endothermic. A thermometer shows whether the temperature rises or falls.",
 [('A reaction makes the test tube feel hot. It is…','exothermic.','endothermic.','neutral.','Energy is given out to the surroundings.'),
  ('Which is an endothermic process?','An instant cold pack.','Burning wood.','A hand warmer.','Cold packs take in energy.'),
  ('How can you measure whether a reaction is exo- or endothermic?','Measure the temperature change with a thermometer.','Weigh the beaker.','Look at the colour only.','Temperature rise = exothermic; fall = endothermic.'),
  ('Combustion is…','exothermic.','endothermic.','not a chemical reaction.','Burning releases energy as heat and light.')],
 [('What is an endothermic reaction?','A reaction that takes in energy from the surroundings, so they get colder.'),
  ('Acid and alkali are mixed; the temperature rises from 20 °C to 27 °C. State the temperature change and the type of reaction.','A rise of 7 °C.~Exothermic.'),
  ('Explain why a polystyrene cup is used when measuring the temperature change of a reaction.','Polystyrene is a good thermal insulator.~It reduces energy transfer to or from the surroundings.~So the measured temperature change is more accurate.')])

ex('catalyst',"Enzymes work best at a certain temperature, often body temperature. High temperatures can denature an enzyme so it stops working. Catalysts save energy in industry because reactions can happen at lower temperatures.",
 [('A catalyst…','speeds up a reaction and is not used up.','slows down a reaction.','is a product of the reaction.','It can be reused.'),
  ('An enzyme is…','a biological catalyst.','a type of acid.','a fat.','Enzymes are proteins made by living things.'),
  ('Why do enzymes stop working at high temperatures?','Their shape changes (they are denatured).','They melt into water.','They become catalysts.','The active site no longer fits.'),
  ('Which change increases the rate of most reactions?','Increasing the temperature.','Using larger lumps.','Diluting the acid.','Particles move faster and collide more.')],
 [('What is a catalyst?','A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up.'),
  ('Give two ways, other than a catalyst, to speed up a reaction between marble chips and acid.','Increase the temperature / use more concentrated acid.~Use smaller pieces (powder) for a bigger surface area.'),
  ('Explain why using a catalyst in a factory is good for the environment and for costs.','The reaction can happen at a lower temperature.~Less fuel is burned, so less energy is used and less carbon dioxide released.~The catalyst is not used up, so it can be reused, saving money.')])

ex('respiration',"Respiration happens in mitochondria in every living cell, all the time. During exercise, you breathe faster and your heart beats faster to supply more oxygen and glucose. Respiration is not the same as breathing.",
 [('Where does aerobic respiration happen?','Mitochondria.','Lungs only.','Nucleus.','Mitochondria release energy in cells.'),
  ('What are the products of aerobic respiration?','Carbon dioxide and water.','Glucose and oxygen.','Lactic acid only.','Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water.'),
  ('Why does your heart rate increase during exercise?','To deliver more oxygen and glucose to muscles.','To cool you down.','To make lactic acid.','Muscles respire faster.'),
  ('Respiration happens in…','all living cells.','only animal cells.','only lung cells.','Plants and animals respire.')],
 [('Write the word equation for aerobic respiration.','Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water (+ energy released).'),
  ('Give two changes in the body during exercise and why they happen.','Breathing rate increases / heart rate increases.~To supply more oxygen and remove more carbon dioxide from respiring muscles.'),
  ('Explain the difference between breathing and respiration.','Breathing is moving air into and out of the lungs.~Respiration is a chemical reaction in cells.~Respiration releases energy from glucose; breathing supplies the oxygen.')])

ex('anaerobic',"In muscles during hard exercise, anaerobic respiration makes lactic acid, which causes fatigue. Afterwards you keep breathing hard to repay the oxygen debt. Yeast respires anaerobically to make ethanol and carbon dioxide, used in baking and brewing.",
 [('What is made in human muscles during anaerobic respiration?','Lactic acid.','Ethanol.','Oxygen.','Glucose → lactic acid.'),
  ('What does yeast make in anaerobic respiration?','Ethanol and carbon dioxide.','Lactic acid only.','Oxygen and water.','This is fermentation.'),
  ('Why does bread rise?','Yeast makes carbon dioxide bubbles.','Yeast makes oxygen.','The flour melts.','The gas gets trapped in the dough.'),
  ('Compared with aerobic, anaerobic respiration releases…','less energy per glucose.','more energy per glucose.','the same energy.','Glucose is not fully broken down.')],
 [('When does anaerobic respiration happen in muscles?','When there is not enough oxygen, e.g. during hard exercise.'),
  ('Write the word equation for anaerobic respiration in yeast.','Glucose → ethanol…~… + carbon dioxide (+ energy released).'),
  ('Explain why a sprinter keeps breathing hard after finishing a race.','During the sprint muscles respired anaerobically, making lactic acid.~Extra oxygen is needed to break down the lactic acid.~This is the oxygen debt, so breathing stays fast until it is repaid.')])

ex('photosynthesis',"Plants use glucose for respiration, to make starch for storage, and to make cellulose for cell walls. The rate of photosynthesis increases with more light, more carbon dioxide or a warmer temperature, up to a limit. Photosynthesis happens in chloroplasts.",
 [('Where in a plant cell does photosynthesis happen?','Chloroplasts.','Mitochondria.','Nucleus.','Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll.'),
  ('Which gas is released by photosynthesis?','Oxygen.','Carbon dioxide.','Nitrogen.','Oxygen is a product.'),
  ('Which increases the rate of photosynthesis (up to a limit)?','More light.','Less carbon dioxide.','Less water.','Light provides the energy.'),
  ('Plants store glucose as…','starch.','fat only.','protein.','Starch is insoluble, so it is good for storage.')],
 [('Write the word equation for photosynthesis.','Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen (using light energy).'),
  ('Give two uses of the glucose made in photosynthesis.','Respiration to release energy / stored as starch.~Making cellulose for cell walls / making proteins or fats.'),
  ('Explain why a plant in a dark cupboard eventually dies.','Without light it cannot photosynthesise.~So it cannot make glucose.~It uses up its stored food in respiration and runs out of energy.')])

ex('leaf',"Inside the leaf, palisade cells near the top are packed with chloroplasts. Air spaces in the spongy layer let gases diffuse. Guard cells open and close stomata to control gas exchange and water loss.",
 [('Why are leaves thin?','Gases only have a short distance to diffuse.','To make them heavy.','To store water.','Short distance = faster diffusion.'),
  ('What are stomata?','Tiny pores that let gases in and out of the leaf.','Roots in the soil.','Seeds.','Usually on the underside of the leaf.'),
  ('Which cells contain the most chloroplasts?','Palisade cells.','Root hair cells.','Guard cells only.','They are near the top where light is strongest.'),
  ('Why are leaves broad and flat?','To absorb as much light as possible.','To lose water faster.','To catch rain.','A large surface area captures more light.')],
 [('What do guard cells do?','Open and close the stomata.'),
  ('Name the gas that enters the leaf for photosynthesis and how it gets in.','Carbon dioxide.~It diffuses in through the stomata.'),
  ('Explain two ways a leaf is adapted for photosynthesis.','Broad and flat to absorb lots of light.~Thin, with stomata and air spaces, so carbon dioxide diffuses in quickly.~Palisade cells packed with chloroplasts near the upper surface.')])

ex('plant-practical',"Move the lamp to different distances and count bubbles per minute. A glass tank of water between the lamp and the plant stops the lamp heating the water. The closer the lamp, the brighter the light and the more bubbles, up to a limit.",
 [('In the pondweed test, what is the independent variable?','Light intensity (distance of the lamp).','Number of bubbles.','Type of water.','You change the lamp distance.'),
  ('What is the dependent variable?','Number of bubbles (or volume of oxygen) per minute.','Distance of the lamp.','Temperature.','You measure the bubbles.'),
  ('Why might a heat shield be placed between the lamp and the plant?','To stop the lamp changing the water temperature.','To make the light brighter.','To add carbon dioxide.','Temperature is a control variable.'),
  ('As the lamp moves closer, the bubble rate usually…','increases.','decreases.','stays zero.','More light, more photosynthesis.')],
 [('Which gas is in the bubbles from the pondweed?','Oxygen.'),
  ('Give two control variables in the pondweed investigation.','Temperature of the water.~Same pondweed / same amount of carbon dioxide (sodium hydrogencarbonate) / same time for counting.'),
  ('Explain why counting bubbles may not be an accurate way to measure the rate of photosynthesis.','Bubbles can be different sizes.~Some oxygen dissolves in the water instead of forming bubbles.~Collecting the gas in a measuring cylinder or syringe would be more accurate.')])

ex('investigate-candle',"Use containers of different volumes and time how long the candle burns. A bigger container holds more air, so more oxygen. Plot volume on the x-axis and burning time on the y-axis.",
 [('What is the independent variable?','Volume of the container.','Burning time.','Candle colour.','You choose the container.'),
  ('Why does the candle go out?','It uses up the oxygen in the container.','The wax runs out at once.','The container gets cold.','Combustion needs oxygen.'),
  ('A bigger container usually makes the candle burn…','for longer.','for a shorter time.','brighter but shorter.','More air means more oxygen.'),
  ('Which is a control variable?','Using the same type and size of candle.','The burning time.','The container volume.','Different candles would be unfair.')],
 [('What does a candle need to keep burning?','Oxygen (from the air).'),
  ('Suggest two safety precautions for the candle investigation.','Use a heatproof mat and keep hair/paper away.~Do not touch the hot container; work with teacher supervision.'),
  ('Explain why the burning time should be measured several times for each container.','Results can vary because of random errors, e.g. reaction time.~Repeats let you spot anomalies.~A mean of repeats is more reliable.')])
